                                                                      📊 Employee Attrition Prediction using Machine Learning


🧠 Project Overview

This project focuses on predicting employee attrition using machine learning techniques. The goal is to identify whether an employee is likely to leave the company based on various features such as job role, income, overtime, and work environment factors.
The project also includes exploratory data analysis (EDA), data preprocessing, handling class imbalance using SMOTE, and model building using Random Forest Classifier.

🎯 Problem Statement
        Employee attrition is a major challenge for organizations. Losing employees leads to increased hiring costs and reduced productivity.


This project aims to:
  Analyze key factors influencing employee attrition
  Build a predictive model to identify employees at risk of leaving
  Improve prediction performance using balancing techniques like SMOTE


📁 Dataset

The dataset contains employee-related attributes such as:
        Age
        Monthly Income
        Job Role
        Overtime
        Stock Option Level
        Marital Status
        Years at Company
        Attrition (Target variable)


⚙️ Technologies Used
        Python\Pandas & NumPy\Matplotlib & Seaborn\Scikit-learn\Imbalanced-learn (SMOTE)


🧹 Data Preprocessing

      Handled missing values
      Encoded categorical variables using One-Hot Encoding
      Scaled/structured features for modeling
      Addressed class imbalance using SMOTE
      📊 Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)

Key insights:
      Majority of employees stay in the company (class imbalance)
      Employees with lower income show higher attrition tendency
      Overtime has a strong relationship with employee leaving behavior

🤖 Model Building
      Model Used: Random Forest Classifier
      Techniques Applied: Hyperparameter tuning
      SMOTE for balancing classes


📈 Model Performance
      Accuracy: ~85%
      Improved recall for minority class using SMOTE
      Balanced performance across classes
              
📊 Feature Importance

The model identified key drivers of attrition such as:
  Stock Option Level
  Monthly Income
  Job Role
  Overtime
  Marital Status


📌 Key Insights

Stock options and income significantly influence attrition prediction
Overtime employees are more likely to leave
Employee satisfaction and compensation are major drivers









